N('rossie-iron-works','Rossie Iron Works: furnace site, foundry and land office','Rossie','nny','industry','ruins',44.38031,-75.65285,'site','view','drive','','1813–1887',
  'David Parish began a furnace at Rossie village in 1813, on ore from the Caledonia mine near Spragueville; the first blast, in 1815, failed, and the works passed through several hands. New stacks went up in 1837 and 1844 (the last 46 feet high, making eleven tons a day), a brick land office that served as bank and pay office was built in 1815, and a stone foundry and machine shop in 1848–49 made kettles, stoves, mill machinery and the first castings for the Northern Railroad. The last blast ended in 1867 and ore shipments to the foundry stopped in 1887. President Monroe came to inspect the furnace in 1817.',
  'In 1956 the town historian wrote that a county road built in 1922 ran through the middle of the furnace site, leaving only a small piece of wall, and that what remained of the works was the well-kept land office, the ruins of the stone foundry, the crumbling walls of the machine shop and the houses of Bembo and Parish. Blacklakeny.com (undated) puts the 1848–49 foundry near the furnace site and a grist mill of 1850 across the river. What stands today was not checked on the ground. A Pomeroy Foundation marker, "Early Mining in NY" (2025), stands at 4 Mill Road. The leaf-off aerial shows a roofless walled building beside the falls just south of Mill Road; that it is the foundry is likely but not confirmed by a source.',
  'The first iron furnace in northern New York, and the village a British raiding party took for one night in 1813.',
  'The land round Mill Road is private (the parking lot OpenStreetMap maps there is not on public land, and the houses are homes). Look from the road; do not go onto the ruins without the owner\'s leave. Not checked on the ground.',
  [SLCHA,L('Black Lake NY: Historic Rossie','https://blacklakeny.com/?p=2542'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Early Mining in NY marker (4 Mill Road)','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/early-mining-in-ny/'),W('Rossie, New York')],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('first-presbyterian-gouverneur','First Presbyterian Church and the Gouverneur Museum (the manse)','Gouverneur','nny','worship','standing',44.3347,-75.4683,'exact','open','drive','','1892–93 (church); 1904 (manse)',
  'The third church of this congregation on its Church Street lot, designed by the Cleveland architect Sidney Rose Badgley and finished in 1893, of rough and smooth Gouverneur marble quarried nearby; its east tower rises 90 feet. The 1904 Queen Anne manse next door is no longer the church\'s: the Gouverneur Historical Association bought it in 1974 and opened it as the town museum on July 4, 1976. Both were listed on the Register in 2015.',
  'A Romanesque church of grey Gouverneur marble with two towers and a five-arched entrance loggia. The Register form (2015) found the interior highly intact: an auditorium-plan worship space under exposed scissor trusses, period plaster and woodwork, stained glass with rectilinear leading, and a pipe organ of 1920. The manse, of yellow pressed brick with a round three-storey tower and a marble base wall, holds the museum on three floors and an exhibit building outside. Church service times not checked.',
  'A whole church built of the local marble, with the village museum in the minister\'s old house next door.',
  'The church is a working church; the museum is at 30 Church Street. Admission free (Town of Gouverneur). The museum\'s hours differ by page: the museum\'s own home page says Wednesdays 1 to 2 p.m., the town\'s page says Wednesdays 1 to 3 p.m.; both say Saturdays by appointment. Street parking on Church Street (not checked).',
  [W('First Presbyterian Church (Gouverneur, New York)'),L('First Presbyterian Church Complex, Register form (15000607)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/15000607_text'),L('Gouverneur Museum','https://www.gouverneurmuseum.com/'),L('Town of Gouverneur: museum','https://www.townofgouverneur.gov/gouverneur-museum'),H(110165,'The First Presbyterian Church and the Gouverneur Museum')],
  brick='no',hours='Museum: Wednesdays 1 to 2 p.m. (museum page) or 1 to 3 p.m. (town page); Saturdays by appointment'),
