N('antwerp-historic-district','Village of Antwerp Historic District','Antwerp','nny','town','standing',44.19806,-75.60917,'area','public','walk','','1805 (settlement); mostly 1840–1900',
  'Gouverneur Morris\'s agents began a dam and sawmill where the road to Long Falls (Carthage) crossed the Indian River in 1805–06, and Ezra Church built the first grist mill there in 1810 (a 19th-century village history). The village that grew round the crossing is nearly all inside a Register district of 2001: 241 contributing buildings, most of 1840–1900, with houses, shops, civic buildings and churches, a memorial park and monument, two cemeteries and a stone arch bridge.',
  'Walk Main Street and the streets off it. The district takes in the stone arch bridge and, by that history, a stone church of the Congregationalists; which buildings survive in what state was not checked building by building. The Civil War memorial (HMdb 32009) stands in the memorial park.',
  'A whole 19th-century North Country mill village on the Indian River, listed nearly entire.',
  'Public streets of the Village of Antwerp, open any time; most buildings are private houses and shops, seen from the street. Street parking (no mapped lot).',
  [W('Village of Antwerp Historic District'),L('History of the Village of Antwerp (19th-century county history, newyorkgenealogy.org transcription)','https://newyorkgenealogy.org/jefferson/history-of-the-village-of-antwerp.htm'),H(32009,'Civil War Memorial, Antwerp')],
  brick='?'),
N('richville-welsh-church','Richville Welsh Congregational Church','Richville','nny','worship','standing',44.40919,-75.39937,'site','view','drive','','1859; renovated 1925',
  'Welsh settlers came to Richville from 1849 on and organised a church in 1858; Ebenezer Griffiths built this one in 1859. Regular services stopped in 1919, and in 1925 his grandson Webster Griffiths renovated the decaying building; a tablet of 1926 lists its pastors and the Welsh families who settled the place. The Richville Welsh Society keeps it.',
  'A small white frame church on Main Street with the 1926 bronze tablet. In 2011 the society held its annual service here on a Sunday late in August. Interior not checked.',
  'A Welsh chapel in the North Country, kept by the settlers\' descendants for one service a year.',
  'Owned by the Richville Welsh Society (the society\'s blog, 2011). Seen from Main Street; inside only at the society\'s annual August service, whose date for this year was not found. Street parking.',
  [H(110169,'Richville Welsh Congregational Church'),L('Richville history blog: annual Welsh church service (2011)','https://richvillenyhistory2.blogspot.com/2011/08/august-28th-200pm-annual-welsh-church.html'),H(110170,'Richville Village')],
  brick='no'),
